{
  "title": "Stacked Exponent Remainder",
  "description": "## Task\n\nBuild a power tower from the list: `nums[0] ** (nums[1] ** (nums[2] ** ...))`, evaluated from the top down, so the last number is the topmost exponent. A tower of one number is that number. Find the tower's value modulo `m`.\n\n## Input\n\n- `nums` — the numbers of the tower, bottom first.\n- `m` — the modulus.\n\n## Output\n\nThe value of the tower modulo `m`.\n\n## Example\n\n```python\nstacked_exponent_remainder([2, 3, 2], 1000)  # returns 512\nstacked_exponent_remainder([5], 3)  # returns 2\nstacked_exponent_remainder([7, 7, 7], 1)  # returns 0\n```\n\n## Constraints\n\n- 1 ≤ length of `nums` ≤ 100000\n- 1 ≤ each number ≤ 10^9\n- 1 ≤ `m` ≤ 10^9\n- The full tower has astronomically many digits, so computing it exactly and reducing afterwards will run out of time or memory.\n",
  "signature": "def stacked_exponent_remainder(nums: list[int], m: int) -> int",
  "topic": "euler tower",
  "difficulty": "hard",
  "category": "math-bits",
